  "raw_reviews": [
    {
      "reviewbody": "Many reviews (including my own) focus on what Jerry was doing, but Phil was also a key part of the improvised counterpoint up until about 1975 (about the time alcoholism took its toll). Both Jerry and Phil do a good Viola Lee, though the New Potato is average for '67.\n\nViola Lee different, in that it is one of the first performances to end in feedback, one of the first extended feedbacks. Through 1966 and much of '67 they ended on the chord. But they had recently performed at the Monterey Pop festival, where Jimi Hendrix shocked the audience with 5 minutes of feedback, during which he burned and smashed his guitar--feedback still going. \n\nInstead of violence during the feed back, the Dead go into campy vaudeville \"outtro\" that they had been ending shows with this year. Unfortunately, it's cut off here.\n\nFrom this Monterey/Hendrix-inspired feedback ending, the Dead would expand feedback into a lengthy \"song\" in it's own right. Over the years, this evolved into the \"space\" that was a standard part of shows for decades.",
      "reviewtitle": "A Hendrix-inspired ending - an early experiment in feedback",
      "reviewer": "L. Rosley",
      "reviewdate": "2008-12-02 01:05:50",
      "createdate": "2008-12-02 01:05:50",
      "stars": "4"
    },
    {
      "reviewbody": "Just listening to anything from Anthem often provides me with that little jolt in the gut as if I were experiancing a flashback.Any live new potato might actually cause me to fail a drug test,this one is no exception!And of course the Viola kicks ass!",
      "reviewtitle": "Flashbacks",
      "reviewer": "donnyraindog",
      "reviewdate": "2012-02-15 21:54:40",
      "createdate": "2012-02-15 21:54:40",
      "stars": "4"
    },
    {
      "reviewbody": "5",
